%[text] %[text:anchor:T_342C4817] # STED Exploration 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] **Stimulated emission depletion** (**STED**) **microscopy** is a [super-resolution microscopy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super-resolution_microscopy) technique. It creates [super-resolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super-resolution) images by the selective deactivation of [fluorophores](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluorophore) to minimizing the area of illumination at the focal point, thereby enhancing the achievable resolution.
%[text] ![](text:image:7804)
%[text] In this example, we will explore the distribution of receptor proteins on the dendrites of olfactory sensory neurons. Each OSN extend a dendrite into the mucosal lining in the sinuses at the back of the noses. Odorants bind the receptors on these dendrites, and active the neurons. 
%[text] ![](text:image:1043)
%[text] This example is based in part on this publication: [Super-resolution imaging of ciliary microdomains in isolated olfactory sensory neurons using a custom two-color stimulated emission depletion microscope](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4923803/).
%%
%[text] ## Read in the images
%[text] In the unit2 data folder, there are two images of the same olfactory sensory neuron:
%[text] - Atto590\_Conf\_1.tif
%[text] - Atto590\_STED\_1.tif \
%[text] One image was taken using a Confocal Microscope, while the other was taken using a STED microscope. These images of olfactory sensory neurons have been stained to detect for the presence of the calcium channel CNGA2 — the α-2 subunit of the CNG channel, part of the signal transduction machinery in olfaction.
clearvars
close all

folder_path = mmSetUnitDataFolder(2); % get the path to the unit2 folder
conf.name = 'confocal';
conf.img = imread(fullfile(folder_path,"Atto590_Conf_1.tif")) % load confocal image %[output:1fb1d31f]

sted.name = 'STED';
sted.img = imread(fullfile(folder_path,"Atto590_STED_1.tif")) % load STED image %[output:315cfae5]
%[text] - notice the images have the same dimensions and are 8-bit images \
%%
%[text] ### Histograms
%[text] Also notice that these images have Very low dynamic range — the max intensity is below 60 
figure; %[output:1e4bf20e]
tiledlayout("vertical") %[output:1e4bf20e]

nexttile %[output:1e4bf20e]
histogram(conf.img) %[output:1e4bf20e]
title(sprintf('%s max = %1.2f',conf.name,max(conf.img(:)))) %[output:1e4bf20e]
xlim([0 60]) %[output:1e4bf20e]

nexttile %[output:1e4bf20e]
histogram(sted.img,40) %[output:1e4bf20e]
title(sprintf('%s max = %1.2f',sted.name,max(sted.img(:)))) %[output:1e4bf20e]
xlim([0 60]) %[output:1e4bf20e]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DE10C7FB] ## Display the Images
%[text] Since the images have such low dynamic dynamic, we will use a modified turbo colormap with 60 colors to better visualize the image contrast
figure %[output:1071b3a3]
tiledlayout(1,2,"TileSpacing","none") %[output:1071b3a3]

nexttile %[output:1071b3a3]
imshow(conf.img, turbo(60)) % display with a 60 color turbo colormap %[output:1071b3a3]
title('Confocal') %[output:1071b3a3]
colorbar("southoutside") %[output:1071b3a3]

nexttile %[output:1071b3a3]
imshow(sted.img, turbo(60)) % display with a 60 color turbo colormap %[output:1071b3a3]
title('STED') %[output:1071b3a3]
colorbar("southoutside") % place the colorbar below the image %[output:1071b3a3]

impixelinfo %[output:1071b3a3]
%[text] - We used a turbo color map with only 48 shades.
%[text] - Notice that there is not a lot of dynamic range in this image \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_97ADF187] ## Find Full Width Half Max
%[text] The full width at half maximum (FWHM) is a parameter commonly used to describe the width of a "bump" on a curve or function. [http://www.wikiwand.com/en/Full\_width\_at\_half\_maximum.](http://www.wikiwand.com/en/Full_width_at_half_maximum.) 
%[text] ![](text:image:9b93)
%[text] Here, we  compare the spatial resolution in the two images by calculating the [full width half max](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_width_at_half_maximum) of  the big dots, which are CNGA2 labels. We will use FWHM to estimate the size of the CNGA2 dots in our images. 
%[text] We will now calculate the FWHM of some dots! The way to do this is to draw a line through one of the dots in the image and calculate the intensity of all of the pixels that touch that line. This is known as a profile line. I have added a local function to the end of this script that will allows us to draw a profile line and calculate the FWHM.
%[text] After executing the function [get\_plot\_FWHM](internal:T_545F2C0C), follow these steps
%[text] 1. FIRST ZOOM in on a region of interest in both plots by clicking on the magnifying glass.
%[text] 2. IMPORTANT: unclick the magnifying icon before proceeding
%[text] 3. You should now be able to draw a line.
%[text] 4. Bisect the center dot that you zoomed in on. The line can be much longer than the diameter of the dot \
%[text] 
sted = get_plot_FWHM(sted) % get fwhm for STED %[output:01f75514] %[output:7c5b10a5]
%[text] - Notice that we have new fields in our ***sted*** structure. These are the measurements captured using the line profile \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1C972E68] ## Now measure confocal spot.
%[text] Repeat the same steps for the corresponding dot in the confocal image image
conf = get_plot_FWHM(conf) %[output:8e10738c] %[output:0b92d1c7]
%[text] - notice that the confocal image is much noisier! \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_50E1A5ED] ## Useful reference
%[text] Notice that we stored information about the line profile in the relevant structure. This allows us to refer back to the pixels of interest without having to redraw the line. 
%[text] Here, for example, here, for both the STED and the Confocal image, we plot a:
%[text] 1. Bounding box onto the original image of the area capture by the line profile
%[text] 2. Cropped close-up of the dot
%[text] 3. Plot of the line profile that you drew \
figure %[output:8ccdaaac]
tiledlayout("flow","TileSpacing","tight","Padding","tight") %[output:8ccdaaac]

% --- Confocal ---
nexttile %[output:8ccdaaac]
imshow(conf.img, hot(40)); % use colormap with fewer rows to emphasize intensity differences %[output:8ccdaaac]
rectangle(Position=conf.rect,EdgeColor='w') %[output:8ccdaaac]
title(conf.name) %[output:8ccdaaac]

nexttile %[output:8ccdaaac]
imshow(imcrop(conf.img,conf.rect),hot(40)) %[output:8ccdaaac]
title('line profile') %[output:8ccdaaac]
hold on %[output:8ccdaaac]
plot(conf.x-conf.rect(1),conf.y-conf.rect(2),'--w') %[output:8ccdaaac]

% --- STED ---
nexttile %[output:8ccdaaac]
imshow(sted.img, hot(40)); % use colormap with fewer rows to emphasize intensity differences %[output:8ccdaaac]
rectangle(Position=sted.rect,EdgeColor='w') %[output:8ccdaaac]
title(sted.name) %[output:8ccdaaac]

nexttile %[output:8ccdaaac]
imshow(imcrop(sted.img,sted.rect),hot(40)) %[output:8ccdaaac]
title('line profile') %[output:8ccdaaac]
hold on %[output:8ccdaaac]
plot(sted.x-sted.rect(1),sted.y-sted.rect(2),'--w') %[output:8ccdaaac]
%[text] - The Signal to noise ratio is very low in this example (very noisy)
%[text] - There is a lot of signal in the background that interferes with our FHWM calculations \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B7DF712F] ## Filter and recalculate FWHM
%[text] The intensity profile for both images were a little bumpy due to the noise of the image. Let's filter the image to clean up some of that noise. Instead of manually redrawing the profile lines, though, we will simply use the lines that you drew in the previous code block on the new filtered images. Notice that we run both a median filter and a gaussian filter on the images first.
% Filter STED
sted.medf = medfilt2(im2uint8(mat2gray(sted.img))); % cleans up salt and pepper noise
sted.gaussf = imgaussfilt(sted.medf,2,'FilterSize',3); % blurs the image
sted.ipf = improfile(sted.gaussf, sted.x, sted.y); % recalculate line profile by inputting previous data

% Filter Confocal
conf.medf = medfilt2(im2uint8(mat2gray(conf.img)));
conf.gaussf = imgaussfilt(conf.medf,3,'FilterSize',3);
conf.ipf = improfile(conf.gaussf, conf.x, conf.y); % recalculate line profile intensities

% Recalculate FWHM
sted.fwhm2 = get_fwhm(1:numel(sted.ipf),sted.ipf);
conf.fwhm2 = get_fwhm(1:numel(conf.ipf),conf.ipf);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3995ED48] ### Display filtered results
figure
tiledlayout(2,2,"TileSpacing","tight","Padding","tight")

% --- Confocal ---
nexttile
imshow(imcrop(conf.gaussf, conf.rect),hot(48));
hold on
plot(conf.x-conf.rect(1),conf.y-conf.rect(2),':c')
title(conf.name)

nexttile
plot(conf.x, conf.ipf,[conf.x(1) conf.x(end)], [max(conf.ipf)/2 max(conf.ipf)/2],'--r' )
title(sprintf('%s FWHM = %4.2f',conf.name, conf.fwhm2))
axis square

% --- STED ---
nexttile 
imshow(imcrop(sted.gaussf, sted.rect),hot(48));
hold on
plot(sted.x-sted.rect(1),sted.y-sted.rect(2),':c')
title(sted.name)

nexttile
plot(sted.x, sted.ipf,[sted.x(1) sted.x(end)], [max(sted.ipf)/2 max(sted.ipf)/2],'--r' )
title(sprintf('STED FWHM = %4.2f',sted.fwhm2))
axis square
%[text] - The Filtered images return a much smoother intensity profile (line trace)
%[text] - FWHM easier to calculate
%[text] - STED has a MUCH smaller FWHM than confocal \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_545F2C0C] # Local Functions
function S = get_plot_FWHM(S)
%%\ plot_FWHM - calculates the full width half max from a profile line drawn on the input image
% INPUT: img - an image
% OUTPUTs: a Structure,S, that contains
%   fwhm - the full width half max
%   line_x - the x coordinates from the profile line
%   line_y - the y coordinates from the profile line
%   rect - a bounding box generated from the coordinates of the profile line

figure(Visible='on',Name=S.name,ToolBar='figure');
tiledlayout(1,3,"TileSpacing","compact")

nexttile; %subplot(rows, cols, 1)
imshow(S.img,hot(25));
title('image')
axis square


%%\ Use the function improfile to get intensities from image
[S.x, S.y, S.ip] = improfile; % get the intensity values from the line

%%\ Display a cropped version of the image and overlay the line

% Create the bounding box of the line you drew
% S.rect = [floor(min(S.line_x)) floor(min(S.line_y)) ceil(range(S.line_x)) ceil(range(S.line_y))];
xy = mean([S.x, S.y]); % centroid
r=ceil(max(range([S.x, S.y]))); % extent
S.rect = [floor(xy)-r/2 r r];


% crop image
nexttile; 
imshow(imcrop(S.img,S.rect),hot(25))
axis square
hold on

% add line
plot(S.x-S.rect(1), S.y-S.rect(2), '--w') % plot the line you drew
title('cropped view')

%%Calculate FWHM
% Create a vector of increasing integers the same size as the vector of intensities from the image
x=1:numel(S.ip);

% calculate the full-width half-max
S.fwhm = get_fwhm(x,S.ip);
%%Display the results by plotting the profile
nexttile; %subplot(rows, cols, 3)
plot(S.x, S.ip, [S.x(1) S.x(end)], [S.fwhm S.fwhm],'--r')
title(sprintf('Full Width Half Max = %4.2f',S.fwhm))
ylabel('intensity')
axis square

end

